    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nAn optional `postcode` on owners, validated only when present: it must be 4 digits, and for a city with a known region it must fall in that region's range (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099); a city with no known region accepts any 4-digit value. Out-of-range or malformed \u2192 400. Absent \u2192 accepted (contract stays backward-compatible). It's stored and returned.\n\n## Changes (kept structurally shallow)\n\nNew, self-contained units (each with ~zero surrounding complexity \u2014 WMC_other \u2248 0):\n- `RequireValidPostcode` \u2014 one small function holding the region-range table and the when-present check.\n- `InvalidPostcodeException` + `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler` \u2014 dedicated checked exception \u2192 400, following the existing escalation pattern.\n- The escalation YAML mapping the exception to its handler.\n\nMinimal edits to existing files, none of which grow an existing method:\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the `postcode` property to `OwnerFields` (so the generated DTO carries it, MapStruct maps it both ways automatically, and it's returned on `GET`).\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 one field + a plain getter/setter (unavoidable for persistence/mapping; no existing method touched).\n- `db/hsqldb/schema.sql` \u2014 one column (the test profile uses `ddl-auto=none`; h2/mysql/postgres use `ddl-auto=update` and pick up the entity field automatically, so they needed no edits).\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `validPostcode` step right after field-requirement, so an invalid body is a 400 before any write.\n\nI deliberately avoided touching the large `OwnerMapper` (MapStruct auto-maps the matching name) and put the new behaviour in fresh small classes rather than expanding `RequireOwnerFields`.\n\n## Verification\n- `AcceptanceTest`: 3/3 pass (`coreStoresValidPostcode`, `coreAcceptsOwnerWithoutPostcode`, `errorRejectsMalformedPostcode`).\n- Full `mvn test`: BUILD SUCCESS.",
